liouville-volume-form-invariance-acd48de.symmetry_origin:
The Liouville volume form is invariant under canonical transformations, which are transformations of coordinates (
 q, p 
) that preserve the form of Hamilton's equations. This invariance is a direct consequence of Liouville's theorem, which states that the total time derivative of the volume element in phase space is zero for a Hamiltonian system. This conservation of phase space volume is a crucial result in statistical mechanics, underpinning the concept of microcanonical ensembles and the microcanonical partition function.

photospheric-timescale.conceptual_definition:
The formula for the photospheric timescale, often denoted as 	au_{\gamma}, quantifies the characteristic time it takes for radiation to diffuse out of the stellar photosphere, which is the visible surface of a star. This timescale is crucial for understanding phenomena like stellar pulsations, supernova light curves, and the evolution of stellar envelopes. It relates the time to the total mass ejected from a star (
 M_{\text{ej}}
 ), the velocity of this ejection (
 v
 ), and a characteristic time (
 t
 ) which is often related to the expansion of the ejecta or other dynamical processes.

photospheric-timescale.symmetry_origin:
The origin of this relationship can be understood from considerations of radiative diffusion through an expanding medium. Radiation pressure and photon mean free path are fundamental to this process. In a simplified diffusion model, the time for a photon to traverse a distance is proportional to the square of the distance divided by the diffusion coefficient. Here, the effective 'distance' is related to the mass of the ejecta (
 M_{\text{ej}} 
 , which scales with the optical depth), and the diffusion coefficient is enhanced by the expansion velocity (
 v 
 ) and the dynamical timescale (
 t 
 ). A more rigorous derivation would involve solving the radiative transfer equation in an expanding geometry, considering the opacity of the stellar material.
